<p>Guests arriving by train tend to fall into two groups: those making the last leg of a longer journey across Mallorca, having boarded an SFM service in Inca, Manacor, or Sa Pobla earlier in the day, and those arriving in Palma itself for the first time on a train that started elsewhere in Spain before a ferry crossing, or on a bus using the same underground complex. Business travellers booked into Hotel Saratoga for meetings or a conference in central Palma are a regular presence on this route too, since the hotel's location on Paseo Mallorca puts them close to the old town's offices and event spaces without needing a second taxi from the station to a further-out business district. For any of these arrivals, having a driver already waiting at a known meeting point removes the one variable that's hardest to plan around from a distance: exactly how busy the station will be, and how easy it will be to find transport, at the specific hour your particular service happens to arrive.</p>
